    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d19">

                <pb facs="0078" n="38" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v25/pg38"/>

                <head>19. National Security Action Memorandum No. 173<anchor n="1" xml:id="d19-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-07-18">July 18,
                    1962</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list type="to">
                    <head>TO</head>
                    <item>The Secretary of State</item>
                    <item>The Secretary of Defense</item>
                    <item>The Attorney General</item>
                    <item>The Chairman, Joint Chiefs of Staff</item>
                    <item>The Director of Central Intelligence</item>
                    <item>The Administrator, Agency for International Development</item>
                    <item>The Director, United States Information Agency</item>
                    <item>The Chairman, Special Group (Counterinsurgency)</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Interdepartmental Field Visits</item>
                </list>
                <p>I feel that there is a need for accelerating our programs for underdeveloped
                    countries and for effecting a closer tie between the County Team planning in
                    these areas and the Washington departments which <pb facs="0079" n="39"
                        xml:id="pg39"/> participate in these programs. It would seem to me that the
                    technique of interdepartmental team visits used last fall in South Viet-Nam and
                    early this year in certain Latin American countries could be broadened and
                    applied to this requirement.</p>
                <p>To this end, I would like to have organized a program of field visits by senior
                    interdepartmental teams, under State Department chairmanship, to a number of
                    selected countries, with particular attention to Latin America. These teams
                    would consult with the Ambassador, his staff and local officials, would review
                    the Country Team plans, and would form a judgment as to their adequacy, their
                    consistency with United States objectives, and the reliability of the
                    intelligence upon which they are based. Upon return to Washington, the teams
                    would submit appropriate recommendations to me, through the Secretary of State,
                    for strengthening United States activities in the countries visited which, when
                    approved, would receive the same kind of intensive implementation as that being
                    accorded the current South Viet-Nam program.</p>
                <p>I would like the Secretary of State, in consultation with the Special Group
                    (Counterinsurgency) to initiate as a matter of urgency a program of visits of
                    the kind outlined above.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">John F. Kennedy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
